  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nAn upper ridge is over the area, and the peak heat is expected to\nbuild in tomorrow afternoon before the ridge breaks down slightly\nfor Monday. The base NBM indicates lows staying above 65 degrees \nfor tonight, Sunday night, and Monday night, so it will be \ndifficult to stay cool enough to sleep comfortably. There are \nsmall wiggles in the upper level flow for various CAMs tomorrow \nafternoon, especially in the west half of the area. Any forcing \nover the west would be enough to generate an intense dry thunderstorm\nsince cloud bases will be very high based on hot surface \ntemperatures. We will keep an eye on this potential, but storm \ncoverage is expected to be low at the very least. There is an \nextreme heat warning in place for the heat from now through Sunday\nevening.\n\nThere is a low (10 percent) chance of a shortwave moving through\nand using the sfc boundaries near or after peak heating to \ngenerate a strong thunderstorm or two today in the far north. \nThere is a similar event expected on Sunday, with this low chance \nstarting mostly in central Montana and moving into our CWA. One \nCAM suggests a severe gust or two with the passage. With near \ninverted Vs on Fcst soundings can not rule this out due to high \nDCAPE (1500 to 2600 J/kg).\n\nThe latest GFS and ECMWF ensemble suggests a toppling of the \nridge crest to the east by late Monday evening, so central \nMontana is expected to start to see precipitation chances again by\nthe time we get to Tuesday afternoon. The base NBM suggests these\nshower and storm chances will continue through late in the\nupcoming work week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nThe main deviation was to winds for tomorrow by using the HRWFV3.\n\nThere is HIGH confidence on temperatures exceeding 95 degrees for\nhigh temperatures and low temperatures struggling to drop below \n65 degrees this weekend, and for highs exceeding 90 on Monday. \n\nThere is MODERATE confidence that fire weather will hold off for \nat least the next week. Recent rains are keeping the vegetation \npretty green, so fire concerns are low.\n\nThere is moderate confidence of an isolated dry thunderstorm \nproducing a 60 mph wind gust on Sunday from 2 PM to 8 PM in the \nwestern part of the area.\n\nThere is HIGH confidence of precipitation chances returning by \nTuesday evening.",
